# Traefik BasicAuth users for the dashboard (compose.yaml mounts the real file,
# config/traefik/users.htpasswd, read-only into the traefik container).
#
# This file is a template and is intentionally comment-only: it carries no
# usable credential. The real file is gitignored and YOU must create it before
# `docker compose up` -- Traefik will not start without it.
#
# Create it (one user, bcrypt, cost 12):
#
#   htpasswd -nbB -C 12 admin '<password>' > config/traefik/users.htpasswd
#
# Add another user (note >>, not >, or you will overwrite the first):
#
#   htpasswd -nbB -C 12 second-user '<password>' >> config/traefik/users.htpasswd
#
# `htpasswd` comes from apache2-utils (Debian/Ubuntu) or httpd-tools (RHEL).
# With no htpasswd installed:
#
#   docker run --rm httpd:2 htpasswd -nbB -C 12 admin '<password>' > config/traefik/users.htpasswd
#
# Each line of the real file looks like `user:<bcrypt hash starting $2y$>`.
# Use bcrypt (-B) at cost 12 (-C 12): htpasswd's default cost is 5, which is far
# cheaper to brute-force. Traefik also reads MD5 and SHA1 entries, but both are weak.
# The price is CPU: cost 12 is roughly 0.3 s per hash on a laptop (cost 10: 0.08 s),
# and a browser sends its credentials with every request. If `docker stats traefik`
# shows it pinned while the dashboard loads, regenerate at -C 10, not the default 5
# (docs/DEPLOYMENT.md, step 4).
#
# Passwords: this login is the only thing between the internet and the dashboard
# and the live video. Use a long random one per person (for example
# `openssl rand -base64 18`), not a word, and do not reuse a password from
# anywhere else. Traefik rate-limits attempts (see compose.yaml) but the file's
# hashes are only as strong as what they hash.
#
# Passwords typed on a command line land in shell history -- prefix the command
# with a space (bash with HISTCONTROL=ignorespace) or use `htpasswd -nB -C 12 admin`
# and type the password at the prompt.
